4. **`negation` (lines 84-88)** vs the formal negation. The formal statement at line 49-55: `forall (s,delta) in D0: exists G comeager: forall data in G: [exists completion ... and complete ...] and forall gamma ...: not exists q ...`. The `not` of that is: `exists (s,delta): forall G comeager: exists data in G: not[conclusion]`, i.e., either no completion, or incomplete generator, or exists visible gamma. The negation text says "there is (Sigma,h,K) in G whose development fails the conclusion: either it admits no conformal completion of the declared kind, or some null geodesic generator of I+ is incomplete, or some future-inextendible causal geodesic of finite affine length is visible from I+." Wait — that's wrong! The not of `forall data in G: P(data)` is `exists data in G: not P(data)`. Yes, exists data in G, not for all G. Actually negation says "for every comeager G there is (Sigma,h,K) in G" — that's not the negation of "exists G comeager forall data in G". The correct negation is "for all G comeager, exists data in G with not-P". Yes that's what it says. OK that's right: ¬(∃G ∀D∈G P(D)) = ∀G ∃D∈G ¬P(D). Good.

But hold on, is the negation normal form consistent? `{ (s,delta) in D0 : { D in X : not P_WCC(D) } is non-meager } is non-empty` — that's the standard statement that the failure set is non-meager for some (s,delta). Is that equivalent to the negation? For a Baire space, if for every comeager G there is D∈G with ¬P, then the set of ¬P is non-meager (otherwise its complement is comeager and would contain no ¬P). Conversely if ¬P set is non-meager, it meets every comeager set. So yes, equivalent in a Baire space. The schema says X_vac is Baire. But the negation text in line 85 says "for every comeager G subset X there is D in G failing" which is the direct negation; the normal form is equivalent given Baire. Fine. Maybe there's a subtlety: the direct negation at line 85 requires all comeager G (including non-Borel ones). The normal form is equivalent in Baire spaces. OK.

